WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:03.520
Sometimes, no matter how great&nbsp;
the rest of the movie is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:08.560
there's that one moment you just keep coming&nbsp;
back to. We're covering the biggest pause-worthy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:14.240
moments in 1990s filmmaking — the funny,&nbsp;
the sexy, and the downright embarrassing.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:19.920
The classic 1999 science fiction film&nbsp;
The Matrix tells the story of a group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:24.000
of people who one day realize that the&nbsp;
world they live in is a simulation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.560
which they then seek to break free from.&nbsp;
Funny enough, a similar thing happened to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:32.960
a small percentage of viewers during a certain&nbsp;
scene in the film, when a small production error&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:38.080
gave them an unexpected reminder that the film&nbsp;
they were watching was itself also a simulation.

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:42.560
"Do you want to know what it is?"

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:48.720
The moment comes when two of our heroes, Morpheus&nbsp;
and Neo, are going to visit the sage-like figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:53.680
known as the Oracle. As they stand in the&nbsp;
hallway outside the door to her apartment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:57.680
there's a gorgeously composed insert&nbsp;
shot of Neo's hand reaching out to grab&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:02.560
the reflective metal doorknob. However, in&nbsp;
addition to reflecting Neo and Morpheus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:06.800
the doorknob is also reflecting the camera&nbsp;
lens. It's the sort of thing that you probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:11.920
won't notice the first few times you watch the&nbsp;
film, but once you see it, you can't unsee it.

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:16.560
That being said, the production team appears to&nbsp;
have come up with a pretty ingenious solution to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:21.600
hide as much of the camera as possible. They've&nbsp;
covered it with a replica of Morpheus' outfit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:25.440
complete with his tie, and they've placed&nbsp;
the camera such a way that it somewhat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:30.640
blends into Morpheus' torso just behind&nbsp;
it. To be honest, unlike some production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:35.200
"goofs," we're genuinely impressed by this&nbsp;
bit of old-school movie-making ingenuity.

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:41.840
When Pulp Fiction introduces us to the two hitmen&nbsp;
characters, Jules Winnfield and Vincent Vega,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:45.600
they're arriving at an apartment building&nbsp;
to retrieve a briefcase that's been stolen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:50.960
by a small-time criminal named Brett ...&nbsp;
and also to execute Brett and his friends.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:55.840
However, before they can finish this job, an&nbsp;
unseen attacker emerges from the bathroom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:59.360
brandishing a pistol. He&nbsp;
opens fire on the two hitmen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:03.520
but once he finishes, we see that&nbsp;
somehow, he hit nothing but air.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:08.480
There are bullet holes in the wall behind them,&nbsp;
but they themselves are totally unharmed. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:14.160
such an unlikely outcome that Jules eventually&nbsp;
comes to believe that this event was a miracle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:19.280
Their lives were spared by God to give them the&nbsp;
chance to repent for all the evil they've done.

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:23.520
"This was divine intervention. You&nbsp;
know what divine intervention is?"

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:27.920
However, some eagle-eyed audience members&nbsp;
noticed a small problem in this scene.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:32.800
If you pause the film during an earlier shot in&nbsp;
the apartment, before the hidden gunman appears,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:36.800
you'll see that the bullet holes are already&nbsp;
present in the wall behind Jules and Vincent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:41.040
Now clearly, this is just a production&nbsp;
error, but if you want to go really far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:45.760
down the rabbit hole of weird headcanons,&nbsp;
there's also an alternate explanation. What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:49.120
if there just happened to be some&nbsp;
bullet holes in that wall already?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:53.360
What if there was no miracle at all, and the&nbsp;
real reason why Jules and Vincent weren't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:57.840
harmed by the gunman's shots was because he&nbsp;
accidentally loaded up his gun with blanks?

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:04.000
The 1999 film Fight Club is one of the&nbsp;
most meticulously constructed movies of all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:07.440
time. And more so than any other&nbsp;
film we're discussing today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:10.640
you'll definitely need to pause the&nbsp;
movie if you want to catch some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:15.440
this film's hidden Easter eggs because some&nbsp;
of them appear in only a single frame each.

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:18.880
We all remember Tyler Durden's&nbsp;
first iconic scene in the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:23.360
right? While on a plane flight home from&nbsp;
a business trip, our unnamed protagonist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:27.680
suddenly realizes that there's a strange&nbsp;
man in red sunglasses seated next to him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:32.320
played to perfection by Brad Pitt. The&nbsp;
stranger then strikes up a conversation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:37.440
and the two discuss airplane safety cards,&nbsp;
homemade napalm, and soap. Although our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:42.880
narrator doesn't realize it yet, this encounter is&nbsp;
about to change the direction of his life forever.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:47.760
But what if we told you that this fateful meeting&nbsp;
isn't actually Durden's first appearance in Fight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:53.040
Club? At several points earlier in the film,&nbsp;
Tyler briefly appears spliced into the film for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:58.320
only a single frame in each appearance, as though&nbsp;
he's flickering in and out of existence. Those of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:02.880
you who've already seen the movie can probably&nbsp;
guess why the filmmakers decided to do this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:06.240
and for those of you who haven't,&nbsp;
we wouldn't want to spoil any of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:10.560
this movie's many delightful twists, so&nbsp;
you'll have to check it out for yourselves.

00:04:11.596 --> 00:04:15.520
The Star Wars films have always presented&nbsp;
landscapes that are rich with surprising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:20.480
visual details and secret Easter eggs.&nbsp;
One of the most famous hidden examples&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:25.920
occurs in the 1999 film Star Wars: Episode&nbsp;
I – The Phantom Menace, but it comes and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:30.080
goes so quickly that you'll probably have&nbsp;
to pause the film to see it for yourself.

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:33.440
The moment occurs midway through the&nbsp;
movie, when the Galactic Senate is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:38.000
meeting on Coruscant . Among the many aliens&nbsp;
that we see gathered together in this meeting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:41.840
are a trio of creatures that will&nbsp;
definitely be familiar to most film fans.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:48.480
They appear to be the same species as the aliens&nbsp;
from the 1982 movie E.T. the Extra-Terrestrial.

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:53.200
This inclusion makes sense once you remember&nbsp;
that the creator of Star Wars, George Lucas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:58.240
and the creator of E.T., Steven Spielberg,&nbsp;
are longtime friends and collaborators.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:02.560
In fact, this is the second crossover&nbsp;
we've seen between these two franchises,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:07.760
since E.T. the Extra-Terrestrial also had a Star&nbsp;
Wars reference in it. When E.T. is wandering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:13.520
around on Halloween, he sees a child in a Yoda&nbsp;
costume. E.T. then starts to approach the child,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:18.640
repeating the word "home" over and over again,&nbsp;
before his human companions drag him away.

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:25.280
The 1997 film Men in Black tells the story of&nbsp;
a secret organization that protects the Earth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:30.080
from extraterrestrial threats and works&nbsp;
to keep the existence of aliens a secret.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:34.160
Shortly after our protagonist, Jay,&nbsp;
is recruited into this organization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:38.400
he's given a tour of the Men in Black's&nbsp;
extensive underground base. Though there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:43.120
are many unforgettable sights he encounters&nbsp;
during this sequence, one shot is so dense with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:48.320
information that audiences had no choice but to&nbsp;
pause the scene if they wanted to take it all in.

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:52.880
Jay comes across a computer terminal that sits&nbsp;
at the base of a massive array of holographic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:58.000
screens, all displaying real-time footage of&nbsp;
various real-world celebrities who are secretly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:04.000
aliens. Among the many celebrities displayed on&nbsp;
the screen are Danny DeVito , Al Roker , Sylvester&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:10.720
Stallone, Dionne Warwick, Newt Gingrich, George&nbsp;
Lucas, and even some Men in Blacks filmmakers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:15.200
including director Barry Sonnenfeld and&nbsp;
executive producer Steven Spielberg.

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:20.480
Now we've arrived at what's probably the&nbsp;
most hotly debated and controversial moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:26.320
we're covering today, a certain infamous "hidden&nbsp;
message" that appears in the sky in The Lion King.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:30.800
The moment comes midway through the film, when&nbsp;
Simba stands contemplatively at the edge of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:36.000
cliff. Overwhelmed by the weight of his past&nbsp;
regrets, he collapses into a tired heap. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:42.000
he does so, his weight sends a cloud of dust and&nbsp;
leaves swirling up into the air. For a few frames,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:47.840
the dust appears to arrange itself into three&nbsp;
letters, interpreted by some viewers as "S-E-X."

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:52.960
Christian groups raised concerns about the moment,&nbsp;
though Disney denied they were actually seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:57.840
anything. Later interviews with animators&nbsp;
reveal that the message was supposed to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:04.480
"SFX," a nod to the film's special effects&nbsp;
department. However, for the 2002 IMAX re-release,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:08.960
and in all subsequent editions of the film,&nbsp;
Disney decided to save itself any more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:14.240
future headaches by just removing the "letters"&nbsp;
entirely, replacing them with abstract swirls.

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:20.880
The 1999 film Cruel Intentions contains&nbsp;
no shortage of sexy, pause-worthy moments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:24.880
but there's one scene in particular&nbsp;
that's gone down in cinema history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:28.480
as one of the steamiest images&nbsp;
ever captured on celluloid.

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:33.280
When the rich and heartless Kathryn discovers&nbsp;
that Court, her former lover, has left her for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:39.120
a girl named Cecile , she comes up with a plan for&nbsp;
revenge. Kathryn befriends Cecile without Court's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:43.680
knowledge and makes it her mission to "corrupt"&nbsp;
Cecile in his eyes by making her sleep with as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:48.400
many other people as possible. One day, when&nbsp;
the two girls are having a picnic in the park,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:52.640
Cecile expresses some hesitance about her new&nbsp;
boyfriend, saying that she's never even kissed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:58.080
a boy before. Kathryn, seeing an opportunity to&nbsp;
help push Cecile towards promiscuity , offers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:02.480
to show her the ropes by "practicing"&nbsp;
with her. Cecile follows Kathryn's lead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:08.720
and after a brief yet passionate make-out, Kathryn&nbsp;
pulls away, leaving Cecile clearly wanting more.

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:11.760
It seems as though audiences&nbsp;
were also left wanting more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:17.600
and this iconic kiss became a moment that many&nbsp;
were fond of pausing and/or rewatching over and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:23.440
over again. It won an MTV Movie Award for Best&nbsp;
Kiss, and has since been parodied many times.

